---
/**
 * Breadcrumb.astro — the ONE breadcrumb on this site.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is the same story every other shared file here tells.
 *
 * There were THREE implementations of a breadcrumb, and the third one said so in
 * its own comment. Measured on the built site at 1440 and 834 on 2026-08-04:
 *
 *   .art__crumb        8 blog posts     ol.meta, generated "/" separator,
 *                                       link box 33.8 x 15 at 1440 and
 *                                       31.2 x 14 at 834 — no target floor
 *   .cmp-breadcrumb    4 compare pages  17px body face, typed "›" separator,
 *                                       min-height: 24px on the link
 *   .cmp-breadcrumb    /compare         a HAND COPY of the one above, in
 *                                       pages/compare/index.astro, whose own
 *                                       comment reads: "This is a second copy
 *                                       of that rule and both should move to
 *                                       one place; recorded rather than done
 *                                       here because the hub page and the
 *                                       article layout do not share a
 *                                       stylesheet yet."
 *
 * That comment is the whole diagnosis. The WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8 argument — that a
 * breadcrumb is standalone navigation and so the inline exemption does not reach
 * it — was written out carefully in TWO of the three places and was simply
 * absent from the third, which is why the blog crumb shipped a 14px target on
 * eight routes. Nobody was careless. There was nowhere to put the rule once.
 *
 * ── THE TRAIL STARTS AT HOME, AND THAT IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE DEFECT ──────
 *
 * /compare rendered "Home › Compare". Its four children rendered "Compare ›
 * CrowMark vs X" — no Home — while the BreadcrumbList JSON-LD on those very same
 * pages emitted "Home > Compare > CrowMark vs X". The page told a reader one
 * trail and a search engine another, from two hand-maintained lists that had no
 * reason to agree. The blog posts did the same thing: "Blog / <category>" on
 * screen, "Home > Blog > <title>" in the graph.
 *
 * So this component takes the SAME array the JSON-LD is built from. There is one
 * list of ancestors per page, it is passed to both, and scripts/check-breadcrumbs.js
 * fails the build if what a browser renders and what the page publishes as
 * structured data ever diverge again.
 *
 * ── WHY `current` IS A SEPARATE PROP RATHER THAN THE LAST ARRAY ENTRY ───────
 *
 * The leaf of a breadcrumb is not a link — it is the page you are on — and it is
 * the one item whose LABEL is a design decision rather than a fact. A blog post's
 * BreadcrumbList leaf must name the page, so it carries the full headline; on
 * screen, in the uppercase mono micro-label this trail is set in, a 45-character
 * headline is a shout, and the category is what a reader actually wants there.
 *
 * Both are correct and they are correct about different things, so the trail —
 * the part that has to agree, because those are the links — is shared, and the
 * leaf is stated once per layout. The gate enforces exactly that split: every
 * LINKED crumb must match the JSON-LD, position for position, and the leaf must
 * be marked `aria-current="page"` and must not be a link.
 */
import type { Crumb } from '../../lib/schema';

/**
 * ── THERE IS NO `class` PROP, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ────────────────────────
 *
 * The first version of this component took one, so a layout could position the
 * block. It does not work, and it fails in the worst possible way: silently, and
 * only for the rules that matter.
 *
 * Astro scopes a <style> block by rewriting every selector to require a
 * `data-astro-cid` attribute that it stamps onto the elements written in THAT
 * template. A child component's root element is not one of those. So a layout
 * passing `class="art__crumb"` gets the class on the <nav> and none of its own
 * `.art__crumb` rules match it. Measured on the built site at 390 on 2026-08-04:
 * layouts/Article.astro hides the crumb below 720px, and with the class passed
 * through it rendered display:block, 24px tall, on every phone.
 *
 * A prop that looks like it positions the block and does not is worse than no
 * prop, so each call site wraps this in an element it owns instead. That also
 * puts the boundary in the right place: WHERE the trail sits is the layout's
 * decision, what it looks like is this file's.
 */
interface Props {
  /** The linked ancestors, Home first. The same array the page gives breadcrumbs(). */
  trail: Crumb[];
  /** The page you are on. Rendered unlinked, as aria-current="page". */
  current: string;
}

const { trail, current } = Astro.props;
---

<nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
  {/*
    `.meta` is the bare mono micro-label from styles/labels.css, carried on the
    <ol> and inherited by every crumb, so this file states no type of its own.
    It is the third of the FOUR shared label recipes and check-treatments.js
    fails the build on a fifth. (It said "third of three" and "fails on a
    fourth" until A-61 added `.grouplabel` on 2026-08-04, which would have sent
    a reader to delete the fourth rather than to stop at a fifth.)
  */}
  <ol class="meta crumb__list">
    {trail.map((c) => (
      <li class="crumb__item">
        <a href={c.path}>{c.name}</a>
      </li>
    ))}
    <li class="crumb__item"><span aria-current="page">{current}</span></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<style>
  .crumb__list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /*
   * THE SEPARATOR IS GENERATED, NOT TYPED. Two of the three implementations this
   * replaces typed a "›" into the markup — one of them inside an aria-hidden
   * span, one of them bare — and a bare one lands in the accessible name of the
   * link beside it. Generated content is never announced and never selectable,
   * which is what a separator should be.
   */
  .crumb__item + .crumb__item::before {
    content: '\203A';
    margin-inline-end: 0.6rem;
    color: var(--c-border-glow);
  }

  /*
   * WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8, stated ONCE. A breadcrumb is standalone navigation, not a
   * link inside a sentence, so the inline exemption does not reach it and the
   * 24px floor applies. `inline-flex` rather than padding so the box grows
   * without moving the text off the baseline the separators sit on, and without
   * changing where the row wraps.
   *
   * The floor is on BOTH axes. Height was the one that failed — 14px on eight
   * routes — but a one-word crumb can be narrow too, and a rule that guards one
   * axis is a rule that has to be revisited the first time somebody writes a
   * crumb called "UK".
   */
  .crumb__item a,
  .crumb__item [aria-current='page'] {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: 24px;
    min-width: 24px;
  }

  .crumb__item a {
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
    /* Not underlined: an index of links read down one edge, not prose. The same
       carve-out styles/links.css makes for everything inside <nav>, and the same
       one check-treatments.js measures with. */
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .crumb__item a:hover {
    color: var(--c-interactive);
  }
  .crumb__item a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .crumb__item [aria-current='page'] {
    color: var(--c-text);
  }
</style>
